  "_readme": "Which cars get a page at /cars/<slug>. The site's popular lists (FIRST_CARS, POPULAR_SUVS, and TRUCKS_AND_FUN in src/lib/car-search.ts) always get one; this file adds other common US models, family cars, and electric cars. Use the catalog's exact make and model names. A page is built only when the Highway Loss Data Institute has claims results for that model (not just an average for its kind of car), for the newest model year our claims data covers; anything else is skipped at build time, and src/lib/seo.test.ts says which. The slug is the make and model in lowercase with dashes (normalizeName in src/lib/catalog-match.ts, so words in parentheses drop out): Toyota RAV4 -> toyota-rav4, Ford F-150 -> ford-f-150, Toyota RAV4 Prime (PHEV) -> toyota-rav4-prime. Slugs have no model year, so a page keeps its address when the data moves to a newer year. Never change a slug once it's live: src/lib/seo-slugs.json pins every live slug, and src/lib/seo.test.ts fails if one changes or disappears. To keep an old address after a catalog rename, add an entry with \"slug\" (this works for cars on the popular lists too; \"why\" is optional for them). To keep a page up for visitors but out of search results and the sitemap, add \"noindex\": true; nothing is excluded automatically. If a car loses its model-level claims data at a refresh, see docs/DATA-REFRESH.md step 7.",
